WEBVTT

00:00:00.161 --> 00:00:03.002
A practical quality workflow has five steps.

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:06.167
First, profile the data. Second, detect potential issues.

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:08.772
Third, investigate the source and context.

00:00:08.772 --> 00:00:14.832
Only then decide whether to correct, retain, exclude, impute, or flag the value.

00:00:14.832 --> 00:00:16.247
Finally, document what happened.

00:00:16.247 --> 00:00:23.702
This sequence creates an audit trail and reduces the risk of silently changing legitimate unusual values.

00:00:23.702 --> 00:00:28.142
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.142 --> 00:00:33.367
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:36.937
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:36.937 --> 00:00:41.607
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team preparing customer, purchase, and web-event data,

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:47.542
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:47.542 --> 00:00:51.832
before discussing syntax.

00:00:51.832 --> 00:00:57.040
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
